$DIR/test.p:73:16: cannot use Reader as T in Send: Reader is not portable: an interface value refers to a type and to data in the sender's address space
    A portable type can be sent between address spaces, such as two operating
    system processes that share no memory. Its values mean the same to the
    receiver as to the sender because they refer to nothing in the sender's
    memory: they are made only of numbers, booleans, enums, packed types, and
    arrays, structs and unions of those.
    Go has one address space, so any value can be passed anywhere and a string
    or pointer always means the same thing. A Plutonium kernel and its processes
    have separate address spaces, and the compiler checks what may cross instead
    of leaving the receiver to read a stale address.
    Instead, send a union with one variant for each type the interface can hold:
        type Shape union {
            Circle struct{ r float64 }
            Rect   struct{ w, h float64 }
        }
    (see "Portable types" in the Plutonium spec, section 8.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2018" for a longer explanation)
